Scion: gfxstream's guest Vulkan driver (Mesa src/gfxstream) on iOS over /dev/vgpu3d, and on the Mac
against the Android emulator's gfxstream. Applied by tools/gfxstream/mk-vk.sh (see VULKAN.md there).

Upstream: Mesa 25.0.7, https://archive.mesa3d.org/mesa-25.0.7.tar.xz (the release tools/mk-mesa.sh
pins), src/gfxstream: gfxstream's guest side, Copyright Google LLC / Android Open Source Project,
SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT (Mesa's licence for those files). The changes below are under the same
licence.

- a darwin platform: platform/scion (copied in from tools/gfxstream/vk/scion), a transport of its own
  (GFXSTREAM_TRANSPORT_SCION: a byte pipe to a "pipe:opengles" render thread, as the GL path uses)
- the core Vulkan commands exported from the library (no loader on iOS); the weak entry points of
  Mesa's tables resolved at load (-undefined dynamic_lookup), as ELF resolves them at link
- no direct mapping when the device cannot map host memory (kParamHostVisible 0): host-visible
  memory is a guest copy, and vkFlushMappedMemoryRanges / vkInvalidateMappedMemoryRanges carry its
  bytes over the stream (gfxstream's own non-direct mode; the host side is unchanged)
- the encoder reads exactly the bytes an invalidate returns (the host rounds its allocations up to a
  page, so for VK_WHOLE_SIZE it sends more than the guest mapped, and the stream went out of step),
  and clamps VK_WHOLE_SIZE with an offset to the rest of the memory
- darwin build fixes in ResourceTracker (drm_fourcc.h from Mesa's include/drm-uapi)

